        {
          "type": "output_text",
          "annotations": [],
          "logprobs": [],
          "text": "{\"translation_en\":\"“Yet I do reproach you for it, Madame,” he replied, “and my reproaches are well founded. Why not see him if he has said nothing to you? But, Madame, he has spoken to you; if his silence alone had revealed his passion to you, it would not have made so great an impression on you. You could not tell me the whole truth; you concealed the greater part of it from me; you even regretted the little you had confessed to me, and you did not have the strength to continue. I am unhappier than I had believed, and I am the unhappiest of all men. You are my wife; **I love you** as my mistress, and I see you loving another man. That other man is the most attractive man at court; he sees you every day, and he knows that you love him. Ah! How could I have believed,” he cried, “that you would overcome your passion for him? I must have lost my reason to have believed it possible.”\",\"larger_context_summary\":\"The Prince de Clèves addresses his wife during the jealous aftermath of her extraordinary confession that she loves another man, whose identity she initially withheld. The unnamed rival is the Duke de Nemours, celebrated at court and himself passionately drawn to the princess. Here the prince reasons that Nemours must have spoken to her and must know of her feelings; he therefore interprets her partial confession and reluctance to continue as concealment. His marriage, his passionate attachment to his wife, and his fear that her love is reciprocated make the confrontation personally devastating. The scene belongs to the escalating jealousy that eventually leads him to have her watched and contributes to his fatal decline.\",\"interpretive_commentary\":\"The target is strongly truth-conditional because the prince presents his love as a fact within an argument: his wife belongs to him by marriage, he loves her passionately, and yet she loves another. “As my mistress” pointedly characterizes that love as erotic and courtly rather than merely dutiful marital affection. The surrounding speech is highly emotional—he calls himself the unhappiest of men and later cries out—but intensity does not by itself make the target a reflexive or expressive discharge. Nor does the marital setting turn it into a promise, vow, or renewed undertaking: the words explain his suffering rather than alter the relationship by convention. Finally, although the confrontation centers on suspected concealment, that possible deception is attributed to his wife. It does not change the core semantic force of his own direct avowal.\"}"
        }
